Research notes for whoever implements this — these are notes to help, not a spec. Please still read the issue and CLAUDE.md yourself and double check anything load-bearing before shipping.

**Confirmed the bug by reading the code directly.** app/lib/time.ts, inside computeCountdown (around line 161):

    let diff = tgtSec - nowSec;
    if (diff <= 0) diff += 86400;

tgtSec = target.h*3600 + target.m*60 - deductSeconds (line ~159) can go negative whenever the buffer exceeds the target's time-of-day in seconds — which only happens when target.h === 0 (checked TimeStepper.tsx: the buffer's minute column caps at 59 and the second column snaps to 5s steps within 0-55, so the max buffer is well under 3600s — smaller than any non-zero target hour, but larger than a small target.m when target.h === 0). When that happens, a single += 86400 isn't enough to land diff back in the positive range, and the bug reproduces exactly as the issue describes (hand-traced the 00:00-target / 59-min-buffer / 23:30 example and got the same -1740 result).

**The suggested fix (single if -> while) is correct and safe:**

    let diff = tgtSec - nowSec;
    while (diff <= 0) diff += 86400;

Bounds check: with tgtSec roughly in [-3599, 86340] and nowSec in [0, 86399], diff before wrapping is roughly in [-89998, 86340]. The while loop never needs more than 2 iterations to land in (0, 86400] — no risk of a runaway loop, it's a fixed, tiny bound.

Do NOT switch to a modulo (((diff % 86400) + 86400) % 86400) — CLAUDE.md is explicit that computeCountdown must "roll past zero to ~86400" (never land on exactly 0), because the stateless passed-cue derivation in app/app/index.tsx (passedAt, ~line 460-472) computes mostRecentFireMs = nowMs - (86400 - cd.total) * 1000 and relies on total never being 0. A modulo would map an exact-target hit to 0 and break that derivation. while preserves the existing "exact hit -> rolls to 86400" behavior exactly.

**Where the fix lives / what it touches:** computeCountdown in app/lib/time.ts is the single source of truth — every caller (app/app/index.tsx passedAt derivation and the active-cue sort around line 2144, app/components/PrimaryCard.tsx, app/components/OnAirView.tsx (3 call sites), app/components/QueuedRow.tsx, app/components/CueEditModal.tsx) goes through this one function, so a one-line change in time.ts fixes all of them. No other call site needs to be touched for the core fix.

**Legacy Luxon updater (app/app/index.tsx, ~line 1136-1161):** checked this — it writes block.countdown into state (assigned/persisted at ~lines 1001, 1167, 1171) but there is no place that actually renders block.countdown (PrimaryCard/OnAirView both use their own computeCountdown call, not this field), so this path is confirmed not user-visible today, matching what the issue says. Its structure differs from the time.ts bug, so it is not the same one-line fix: it adds a day (if (targetDT <= nowInZone) targetDT = targetDT.plus({days:1})) BEFORE subtracting the deduction, so a large deduction can push it negative again AFTER the wrap-check already ran. If this is aligned too (optional per the issue, not required for the core fix), it needs its own re-check/loop AFTER the deduction is subtracted, not a copy-paste of the time.ts line. Given it's dead/unused and CLAUDE.md favors minimal changes, leaving it alone unless told otherwise seems reasonable — flagging so it's a conscious choice either way.

**Testing gotcha worth knowing up front:** this repo has no unit test framework wired up for app/ (no jest/vitest config, no existing *.test.ts files) — the only automated tests are the Gemini-driven AI E2E harness in tests/ai/, which drives the real running app against real wall-clock time and has no scenario touching this. Reproducing the exact bug window (needs the viewer's clock to actually be in the late-evening range, e.g. 23:01+ for a 59-min buffer on a 00:00 target) isn't practical to script as a new AI E2E scenario without clock manipulation, wh
